"Counting people for the House raised a harder question. The Constitution counted each enslaved person as three fifths of a person when dividing seats, and it excluded “Indians not taxed.” The three-fifths rule gave the slaveholding states more seats in the House, and therefore more electoral votes, than their free population alone would have earned, while the people counted had no vote at all. After the Civil War, the Fourteenth Amendment replaced the rule: seats would be based on “the whole number of persons in each State,” though it still excluded “Indians not taxed.” The original compromise over representation cannot be told honestly without this part.": "5f62426af5b5",
"House seats are apportioned among the states after each census.": "5cafbe76e256",
"How the Senate's equal vote should be judged is still argued. Both sides start from the same facts: in 2020 the most populous state had more than 68 times as many residents as the least populous, and each has two senators. Supporters say the design protects smaller states, makes laws win support across many regions, and reflects a union of states as well as of people. Critics say it gives some voters far more weight than others, lets senators representing a minority of Americans block a majority, and applies a rule made for thirteen states to fifty. This lesson presents both arguments without deciding between them.": "6e2e6dc46e6b",
"In the Congress of the Articles of Confederation, each state's delegation cast one vote, whatever the state's size. When delegates met in Philadelphia in 1787, the large states wanted that rule gone. The Virginia Plan, presented on May 29, proposed a legislature of two branches, with votes in proportion to each state's wealth or free population. On June 15 the New Jersey Plan answered by proposing to strengthen the existing Congress, where each state kept its single vote. For weeks the convention argued over this one question, and several delegates feared it would break up the meeting before a constitution could be written.": "69bef308c2a5",
"In the Congress of the Articles of Confederation, each state's delegation cast one vote, whatever the state's size. When delegates met in Philadelphia in 1787, the large states wanted that rule gone. The Virginia Plan, presented on May 29, proposed a legislature of two branches, with votes in proportion to each state's wealth or free population. On June 15 the New Jersey Plan answered by proposing to strengthen the existing Congress, where each state kept its single vote. For weeks the convention argued over this one question, and several delegates feared it would break up the meeting before a constitution could be written. The answer, adopted on July 16, 1787, is usually called the Great Compromise or the Connecticut Compromise, after its architects, the Connecticut delegates Roger Sherman and Oliver Ellsworth. One chamber, the House of Representatives, would be apportioned by population. The other, the Senate, would give each state an equal vote; a week later, on July 23, the delegates settled on two senators per state. The vote was five states to four, with Massachusetts divided, and it was taken by state delegations, not by individuals. The large states had won one chamber; the small states had kept equality in the other. The two chambers were also designed to differ in character. Representatives serve two-year terms, so they must face the voters often. Senators serve six-year terms, and only about a third of the Senate is elected every two years, so the chamber changes slowly. A representative must be at least 25 years old and a citizen for seven years; a senator, at least 30 and a citizen for nine. The framers expected the House to reflect the mood of the people quickly and the Senate to be steadier. Both must pass the same bill, in the same words, before it can become law. Counting people for the House raised a harder question. The Constitution counted each enslaved person as three fifths of a person when dividing seats, and it excluded “Indians not taxed.” The three-fifths rule gave the slaveholding states more seats in the House, and therefore more electoral votes, than their free population alone would have earned, while the people counted had no vote at all. After the Civil War, the Fourteenth Amendment replaced the rule: seats would be based on “the whole number of persons in each State,” though it still excluded “Indians not taxed.” The original compromise over representation cannot be told honestly without this part. Senators were not at first chosen by the voters. The Constitution gave that power to the state legislatures, partly so that the states as governments would have a voice in the national government. Over time, deadlocked legislatures left Senate seats empty, and many Americans came to see the system as indirect and open to corruption. The Seventeenth Amendment, ratified in 1913, provided that senators would be “elected by the people” of each state. The equal vote of every state in the Senate did not change, and Article V protects it: no state may lose that equality without its own consent. The size of the House is set by law, not by the Constitution. The first House had 65 members. It generally grew with the population, though it shrank once, after the 1840 census, until 1911, when Congress set the number at 433, with one seat added for each of the two territories expected to become states. Since 1913 the House has had 435 voting members, except from 1959 to 1963, when it briefly had 437 after Alaska and Hawaii joined the Union. After each census the 435 seats are divided among the states by a formula, and every state receives at least one. After the 2020 census, California had 52 seats, and six states had one each. The two chambers also hold different powers. Bills for raising revenue must start in the House. The House alone can impeach a federal official, and the Senate alone can try the case, with conviction requiring two thirds of the senators present. The Senate also gives its “advice and consent” to treaties, which need two thirds of the senators present, and to the president's nominations of judges and other officers. Each chamber writes its own rules. For example, under Senate rules, ending debate on most bills takes sixty votes, so more than forty senators can keep a bill that a simple majority supports from coming to a vote. How the Senate's equal vote should be judged is still argued. Both sides start from the same facts: in 2020 the most populous state had more than 68 times as many residents as the least populous, and each has two senators. Supporters say the design protects smaller states, makes laws win support across many regions, and reflects a union of states as well as of people. Critics say it gives some voters far more weight than others, lets senators representing a minority of Americans block a majority, and applies a rule made for thirteen states to fifty. This lesson presents both arguments without deciding between them.": "9955f0690470",
